// Backdoor IAM User with Federated Token (T1550.001)
//
// GetCallerIdentity is deliberately ABSENT. It is among the highest-volume calls
// in AWS and bundling it, as the original query did, buries the rare
// GetFederationToken signal entirely.
//
// Scored on the two things that determine how bad a minted token is: how BROAD
// the requested policy was, and how LONG it lasts (up to 36h / 129600s).
//
// requestParameters.policy IS LOGGED UNREDACTED, the attacker's requested
// policy document is available for forensics, which is unusually generous.
//
// Dialect: Sentinel / Azure Log Analytics KQL. NOT CloudWatch Logs Insights.
AWSCloudTrail
| where TimeGenerated > ago(7d)
| where EventSource == "sts.amazonaws.com"
| where EventName == "GetFederationToken"
| extend Req  = parse_json(RequestParameters)
| extend Resp = parse_json(ResponseElements)
| extend SessionName  = tostring(Req.name)
| extend DurationSec  = toint(Req.durationSeconds)
| extend RequestedPolicy = tostring(Req.policy)
| extend FederatedArn = tostring(Resp.federatedUser.arn)
| extend MintedKey    = tostring(Resp.credentials.accessKeyId)
| extend BroadPolicy  = RequestedPolicy contains "\"Action\":\"*\""
                     or RequestedPolicy contains "\"Action\": \"*\""
| project TimeGenerated, UserIdentityArn, SessionName, DurationSec, BroadPolicy,
          FederatedArn, MintedKey, SourceIpAddress, ErrorCode
| extend Verdict = case(
    isnotempty(ErrorCode),                  "GETFEDERATIONTOKEN DENIED, probing",
    BroadPolicy and DurationSec >= 43200,   "BROAD LONG-LIVED FEDERATION TOKEN - P0",
    BroadPolicy,                            "BROAD FEDERATION TOKEN - P0",
    "FEDERATION TOKEN MINTED, verify against approved federation use")
| order by TimeGenerated desc
//
// ============================================================================
// IMPORTANT, what Action:"*" actually means here
// ============================================================================
// The token's permissions are the INTERSECTION of the source user's permissions
// AND the inline policy. So Action:"*" is a CEILING, not a grant, it is NOT
// privilege escalation. The blast radius is exactly the source user's own
// privilege. Triage accordingly: a broad policy on a low-privilege user is far
// less serious than a narrow one on an admin.
//
// ============================================================================
// REVOCATION, the part that catches responders out
// ============================================================================
// There is NO API to revoke a federation token. Rotating or deleting the source
// user's ACCESS KEYS DOES NOT STOP IT, the token was already minted.
//
// The only reliable path is to attach an explicit DENY to the SOURCE IAM USER.
// Permissions are evaluated per-request against the user's live policies, so
// the deny cascades to the derived federated session near-immediately (IAM is
// eventually consistent; allow a few seconds for propagation).
// Deleting the user also ends its federated sessions.
//
// HUNT what the token did, pivot on the minted key (starts with ASIA):
//
//   AWSCloudTrail
//   | where TimeGenerated > ago(7d)
//   | where UserIdentityAccessKeyId == "<MintedKey>"
//        or (UserIdentityType == "FederatedUser" and UserIdentityArn has "federated-user/<SessionName>")
//   | summarize Calls = count(), Actions = make_set(EventName, 30),
//               IPs = make_set(SourceIpAddress, 10)
